{"abstract":"Machine Learning (ML) models are increasingly applied to wearable self-tracking technologies to offer daily classifications and recommendations for well-being. This shift introduces design challenges, particularly regarding the opacity of training processes and model outputs. We contribute to this space with a conceptual framing of the algorithmic gaze on body and well-being, which we use to critically investigate long-term engagement with a wearable self-tracking technology. Through an autoethnographic study with the Oura Ring, we identified three themes, highlighting tensions between wearer and the ML models, namely: Conflicting narratives of daily activities, fine-tuning of the human, and blurry boundaries of multiple bodies using such devices simultaneously. Departing from the themes, we used fabulation as a method to craft narratives that probe the tensions from the algorithmic gaze, from which we offer alternative design openings for ML in wearable self-tracking devices.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Meyer, Louie Søs","Tsaknaki, Vasiliki"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-861032"],"event":{"end":"2026-04-17","place":"Barcelona, es","start":"2026-04-13","title":"Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems","year":"2026"},"filters":{"added":"2026-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Fabulation","Machine learning","Self tracking Technologies","Wearables"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring and probing the algorithmic gaze on bodies and well being","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3772318.3791795","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-861032","identifier":[{"id":"105038753452","